{hero} 

headline: The Federal Infrastructure Funding Tracker 

date: Last updated October 21, 2026 

{}

 

[+intro_before_map] 

 

Communities nationwide benefit from federal investments to maintain and expand vital infrastructure, including transportation, housing, energy, water, and broadband. Though many of these investments have historically improved community well-being, some federal support has left other communities behind.

As federal policymakers have allocated more funding for infrastructure projects in recent years, new questions must be asked: Where have federal infrastructure dollars been spent? And did those dollars reach the communities that most need funding? 

[] 

{intro_map} 

header: Where Was Funding Distributed? 

Description: Funding amount per 1,000 residents ($)  

county_note: In some cases, a funding obligation for a statewide effort may list the state government as the recipient and the state capital as the recipient location. While we have taken steps to account for this, there may still be cases in this tool where counties that contain state capitals appear to receive more money than they actually received.

cbsa_note: In some cases, a funding obligation for a statewide effort may list the state government as the recipient and the state capital as the recipient location. While we have taken steps to account for this, there may still be cases in this tool where CBSAs that contain state capitals appear to receive more money than they actually received.

{} 

[+intro_after_map] 

We analyzed dozens of federal funding initiatives to determine how much federal infrastructure money communities have received and whether that funding has met community needs. This tool includes many of the most significant infrastructure programs enacted through the <a href="https://www.congress.gov/bill/117th-congress/house-bill/3684/text" target="blank">Infrastructure Investment and Jobs Act</a>, the <a href="https://www.congress.gov/bill/117th-congress/house-bill/5376/text" target="blank">Inflation Reduction Act</a>, the <a href="https://www.congress.gov/bill/117th-congress/house-bill/4346" target="_blank">Creating Helpful Incentives to Produce Semiconductors</a> (CHIPS) Act, and other laws, but it is not exhaustive. 

[] 

