prompt: Search for a type of infrastructure or a geography.

placeholder: Search

explainer_subhead: Not sure where to start? 

[+.explainer_text] 

Try searching for a state, county, metropolitan area, or micropolitan area (<a href="[base]/state/17/" target="_blank">Illinois</a>, <a href="[base]/county/17031/" target="_blank">Cook County</a>, or <a href="[base]/cbsa/16980/" target="_blank">Chicago</a>, for example) to see if it receives funding proportional to its needs. Or try searching for an infrastructure category (like <a href="[base]/program-categories/public-transportation/" target="_blank">public transportation</a>) to see if the programs in that category distribute funds in a way that meets community needs. 

 

Some infrastructure projects are competitively funded, meaning federal officials review applications from prospective grantees and then choose where to direct money. Other projects are formula funded, meaning states and localities distribute a pot of federal money allocated to them by Congress or federal agencies. For more information on funding mechanisms, see our <a href="[base]/user-guide/" target="_blank">user guide</a>.  

