{category_page} 

header: Infrastructure Categories 

about_text: We summed program-level infrastructure investments into nine distinct infrastructure categories. In total, we identified 71 programs funded through the Infrastructure Investment and Jobs Act, the Inflation Reduction Act, and annual appropriations. We then assigned each program to the most relevant infrastructure category and analyzed whether this category-level funding met community needs based on a variety of characteristics.
 

agency_text: Funded by [agencies]. 

overview_text: Programs that address <strong>[category_name]</strong> offer funds to [description]. 

{.takeaways}

ports: Airport and dock programs tend to fund areas with existing airports and docks, rather than investing in new areas.

broadband: Broadband programs direct more funding to places with less internet access and lower broadband speeds.

environmental_resilience: Environmental resilience programs direct more funding to places with low incomes, such as counties that have experienced persistent poverty. These programs provide less funding to areas with higher ozone concentration, a form of air pollution.

energy_and_power: Energy and power programs direct similar levels of funding to places with high household energy costs as to places with lower household energy costs.

housing_and_community_development: Housing and community development funds are distributed more often to places with higher housing cost burdens and more overcrowding. They are also more likely to go to communities with a higher share of people of color. Communities with lower housing staff capacity receive less funding.

roads_and_bridges: Funding for roads and bridges tends to go to places that are heavily reliant on car travel already. These communities have more highways, their residents drive more, and drivers experience higher rates of fatal crashes. Places with a higher share of people of color receive less funding.

passenger_and_freight_rail: Passenger and freight rail funds are disproportionately distributed to places without intercity passenger rail service. In addition, they are more likely to go to counties that experience disproportionate environmental or socioeconomic burden.

public_transportation: Public transportation funding tends to go to places with lower transportation costs and greater access to employment. These funds also disproportionately go to communities where commuters are more likely to take transit, where households are less likely to own cars, and where families have higher incomes.

water_conservation: Water conservation programs send more funds to places with more water system violations.

{}

obligated_funds_caption: Obligated funds are those that the federal government has promised to pay through a binding agreement, such as an official contract or purchase order.

 

{.data_viz} 

header_equity: How Were Funds Distributed to Communities with the Greatest Needs? 

equity_description: Every community has different infrastructure needs. Those with worse infrastructure, poorer residents, or less local government staff capacity may need more funding to achieve the same results than areas with more resources. Here, we compare the amount of federal infrastructure funding obligated to different types of high-need communities.

disadvantaged_text: Disadvantaged counties, those that experience disproportionate environmental or socioeconomic burden, received [value] percent of total obligated funding, a <strong>[higher/lower]</strong> share of [category name] funding compared with their share of the nation’s population (23.5 percent).

persistent_poverty_text: Counties where more than 20 percent of the population has lived in poverty for at least three decades received [value] percent of total obligated funding, a <strong>[higher/lower]</strong> share of [category name] funding compared with their share of the nation’s population (5.5 percent).

concentration_table_title: Funding Across the Highest-Need Communities

concentration_section_description: Federal programs are meant to address communities’ infrastructure needs, but those programs may serve some needs better than others. Here, we compare several characteristics that can indicate a community’s level of need for the selected infrastructure category with the federal funding distributed to those communities. These comparisons can help stakeholders understand where federal funding meets community needs and where it may not.

concentration_table_description: We define highest-need communities as those that rank highest according to a given characteristic and cumulatively contain 25 percent of the US population. Based on their share of the population, we would expect these highest-need communities to receive 25 percent or more of all obligated funding.

concentration_box_title: Magnitude of Funding Received by Highest-Need Communities, Compared with Baseline Expectation (25 Percent)

header_map: Where Was Funding Distributed? 

subhead_map: Funding amount per 1,000 residents

county_note: In some cases, a funding obligation for a statewide effort may list the state government as the recipient and the state capital as the recipient location. While we have taken steps to account for this, there may still be cases in this tool where counties that contain state capitals appear to receive more money than they actually received.

cbsa_note: In some cases, a funding obligation for a statewide effort may list the state government as the recipient and the state capital as the recipient location. While we have taken steps to account for this, there may still be cases in this tool where CBSAs that contain state capitals appear to receive more money than they actually received.

header_quintile: How Were Funds Distributed by Community Characteristic? 

quintile_description: For each infrastructure category, we analyzed how funding was distributed along different community characteristics. For counties and metropolitan areas, we show these funding distributions by quintiles, and for states, we show quartiles. For more information, refer to our <a href="[base]/user-guide/" target="_blank">user guide</a>.

